@page "/learn/renderfragment-across-render-modes"
@layout LearnLayout

<PageTitle>Static content in interactive components · Field Guide</PageTitle>

<article class="l-article" data-learn>
    <h1 class="l-title">Static content in interactive components</h1>
    <p class="l-sub">You've built an interactive card. Why does a static page fail when it puts a table inside it?</p>
    <p class="l-meta">.NET 11 · Rendering · Static SSR with an Interactive Server card · about 5 minutes</p>
    <p class="l-new">
        <b>What's new in .NET 11:</b> a static page can put markup inside an interactive component (its
        <code>ChildContent</code>, or any other <code>RenderFragment</code> parameter). In .NET 10 the same code compiled, then failed every
        request with a 500: <i>"RenderFragment, which is arbitrary code and cannot be serialized"</i>. There's no new
        API. The fix is inside the framework.
    </p>

    <p>
        Below is a <i>Warehouse</i> page. It's static: the server renders it once, with no circuit (the live
        connection an interactive component uses). It lists products that are low on stock, inside
        <code>Collapsible</code>, a small card that runs on Interactive Server so its header can fold it away.
    </p>
    <p>
        You don't need a circuit just to fold content: a <code>&lt;details&gt;</code> element does that. Think of
        the card as a stand-in for something that does need C#, like tabs that load on demand or a dialog with its own
        state. What matters is the setup: an interactive component with static markup inside it.
    </p>
    <p>
        The .NET 10 failure can't happen on .NET 11, so Fig. 1 replays what our .NET 10 build returned for the same
        page.
    </p>

    <DemoFigure Number="1" Title="The page, as .NET 10 ran it" Url="demo/static-content/low-stock/net10"
                Label=".NET 10 · recorded" Side="before">
        <Caption>
            This is a recording, so its buttons do nothing. It replays <code>GET /warehouse</code>, our .NET 10 copy of
            the page in Fig. 3. The build gave no warnings.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <h2><small>The problem</small>.NET 10 can't send code across</h2>
    <p>The table between the card's tags isn't data. It's a <code>RenderFragment</code>: a piece of code that writes markup.</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What the static page tries to pass to the card</span>
        <pre class="snip">Collapsible  <span class="dim">@@rendermode="InteractiveServer"</span>
├─ Title         <span class="dim">string</span>          <span class="ok">✓ serialized, sent to the circuit</span>
└─ ChildContent  <span class="dim">RenderFragment</span>  <span class="err">a delegate: arbitrary code</span>
   └─ &lt;p&gt; Queried at, &lt;table&gt; ...</pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        The static page's HTML is sent to the browser first. The card only comes alive after that, over the circuit,
        so its parameters have to be written down as data and sent along. A string can be written down. Code can't. .NET 10 checks this while the static page renders, and throws
        <code>InvalidOperationException</code>: <i>"which is arbitrary code and cannot be serialized"</i>. The build
        doesn't warn you.
    </p>

    <h2><small>The fix</small>Nothing to change</h2>
    <p>The page that failed on .NET 10 now works. Pass markup to an interactive component as usual:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">The whole page, in short</span>
        <pre class="snip"><span class="hl">&lt;Collapsible @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" Title="Low stock"&gt;</span>
    &lt;p&gt;Queried at @@queriedAt&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;table&gt;...&lt;/table&gt;
<span class="hl">&lt;/Collapsible&gt;</span></pre>
    </div>
    <p>
        .NET 11 doesn't send the code. The static page runs the fragment while it renders, keeps the markup it
        produced, and sends that to the card. The card then shows it like any other <code>ChildContent</code>.
    </p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">What gets lost, without an error</span>
        <pre class="snip">&lt;Collapsible @@rendermode="InteractiveServer" Title="Low stock"&gt;
    &lt;button <span class="err">@@onclick="Reorder"</span>&gt;Reorder&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/Collapsible&gt;</pre>
        <div class="verdict bad">✗ the button shows, but its click handler is dropped. Only a warning in the server log tells you. The same goes for <code>@@ref</code> and <code>@@formname</code>. Put buttons that run C# inside the interactive component, not in the markup you pass it.</div>
    </div>
    <p>Here's the same page, live on .NET 11.</p>

    <DemoFigure Number="2" Title="The same page, on .NET 11" Url="demo/static-content/low-stock"
                Label=".NET 11 · live">
        <Caption>
            The page loads, table and all. Click <b>▾ Low stock</b> to fold the card, and again to reopen it. The
            click runs in C# on the server. <b>Queried at</b> doesn't change, because the page's query didn't run again.
        </Caption>
    </DemoFigure>

    <p>Here's the page, then the card:</p>

    <CodeFigure Number="3" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/StaticContent/LowStock.razor" Feature="renderfragment-across-render-modes">
        <Caption>
            The page Fig. 2 runs, unchanged. Fig. 1 replays our .NET 10 copy of it. The highlighted lines aren't a
            change. They mark the content passed to the card.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <CodeFigure Number="4" Path="Components/Guide/Demos/StaticContent/Collapsible.razor" Feature="renderfragment-across-render-modes">
        <Caption>
            The card, also unchanged. The highlights mark the <code>ChildContent</code> parameter and where it shows.
            The card starts open. If it started folded, its content wouldn't render when the page does, so there'd be no
            markup to keep.
        </Caption>
    </CodeFigure>

    <h2><small>The .NET 10 way</small>Make the page interactive, or move the table</h2>
    <p>With no way to pass the table in, you had two choices:</p>
    <div class="fig">
        <span class="fig-title">Two ways round it in .NET 10, and what they cost</span>
        <div class="flow">
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">@@page "/warehouse"
<span class="err">@@rendermode InteractiveServer</span></pre>
                <span class="why">the whole page is interactive, so the table no longer has to be sent to the card, and the card folds and reopens</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ every visitor keeps a circuit open for the whole page. The table's code reruns on the server whenever the page re-renders, so a timestamp in it keeps changing</div>
            </div>
            <div class="box">
                <pre class="snip">&lt;LowStockCard @@rendermode="InteractiveServer"
              <span class="err">Products="products"</span> /&gt;</pre>
                <span class="why">the table moves into the interactive component, which takes the data instead</span>
                <div class="verdict bad">✗ a new card for every table, each with its own copy of the folding logic</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
    <p>
        Either way, you couldn't have one reusable interactive wrapper around static content from many pages. The
        content had to become interactive too, or move inside the wrapper.
    </p>

    <h2><small>.NET 11</small>The card gets a snapshot</h2>
    <p>
        In .NET 11 the content's code runs once, during the static render. The card only shows the result. That's why
        <b>Queried at</b> in Fig. 2 never changes. It also means the content can't update: anything that should change
        while the user watches has to load inside the card.
    </p>

    <h2><small>Good to know</small></h2>
    <ul class="gk">
        <li><b>It must render the first time.</b> With <code>prerender: false</code>, or content inside an <code>@@if</code> that's false when the page renders, there's nothing to capture, and the page throws when it runs. The build can't catch this. That's why the card starts open.</li>
        <li><b>Only plain <code>RenderFragment</code> works.</b> A <code>RenderFragment&lt;T&gt;</code>, like a grid's row template, still throws, as in .NET 10.</li>
        <li><b>Watch the size.</b> The captured markup is sent to the server when the circuit starts, packed in a form several times bigger than the HTML the visitor sees. The circuit runs on SignalR, which limits a message to 32 KB by default. This app's dashboard puts two tables in cards like this and went over it, so <code>Program.cs</code> raises <code>MaximumReceiveMessageSize</code> to 64 KB. A short table is fine; a long report isn't.</li>
    </ul>
</article>
